+# August 19 and August 31 Moussaoui memoranda: distinct purposes, shared information and unresolved court-copy limits
+
+The official court index lists **two August 19 memoranda**, exhibits 688 and 692. Both were acquired and all 12 page images read, then compared with all five images of the unchanged August 31 exhibit 810. These are court-hosted representations of FBI documents, not authenticated native authoring or delivery records. The comparison identifies what these copies say; it does not establish attack foreknowledge, suppression, a coordinated purpose or the full drafting lineage.
+
+## Source and purpose comparison
+
+| Court exhibit | Date on opening sheet | Actual PDF pages | Stated purpose and precise locator |
+|---|---|---:|---|
+| [688](https://coop.vaed.uscourts.gov/moussaoui/exhibits/defense/688.pdf) | August 19, 2001 | 5 | Physical page 1 requests permission to contact the Minnesota U.S. Attorney's Office to pursue a parallel criminal matter. A request is not evidence that permission was granted or denied. |
+| [692](https://coop.vaed.uscourts.gov/moussaoui/exhibits/defense/692.pdf) | August 19, 2001 | 7 | Physical pages 1 and 7 request identifying information and information about activities/known associates, particularly in the United Kingdom. This is not evidence of actual transmission to or action by any particular recipient. |
+| [810](https://coop.vaed.uscourts.gov/moussaoui/exhibits/defense/810.pdf) | August 31, 2001 | 5 | Physical page 1 states a purpose of advising FAA about a potential threat to commercial-aircraft security. Physical page 4 / printed 6 contains an explicit threat-assessment heading. Purpose is not proof of receipt. |
+
+Different purpose statements are observed facts. They do not establish an author-approved revision chain or prove that each document replaced another. In particular, exhibit 692's seven-page length must not be used to identify it as the later seven-page local FAA-warning memorandum described by OIG.
+
+## Concerns already present in the August 19 copies
+
+Exhibit 688 physical page 5 invokes suspected federal terrorism and aircraft-destruction offenses, including 18 U.S.C. sections 2332b and 32, as investigative theories. Exhibit 692 physical page 7 expresses a terrorism concern and a belief in possible UK connections, then requests further information. These are investigators' stated beliefs and requests, not our findings that an identified plan or network actually existed. They prevent describing the August 31 copy as the first appearance of any terrorism concern in these held papers.
+
+The instructor accounts concerning aircraft doors, the mode-control panel, training experience and a proposed Heathrow-to-JFK simulator exercise recur across the copies. Specific matches are in [COMPARISON.json](COMPARISON.json): 688 physical pages 2/4; 692 physical page 2; 810 physical page 2. Repeated accounts in related memoranda are not independent measurements of aircraft capability or independent witness corroboration. A hypothetical simulator route is not an established operational target or attack route.
+
+## What is different in the August 31 copy
+
+Exhibit 810 physical page 4 / printed 6 explicitly states Minneapolis's assessment concerning preparation to seize a 747-400 for a terrorist act. The same passage says the extent of the plan was unknown. The following passages, continuing onto physical page 5 / printed 7, concern uncertainty about detention/property retention in France and possible future training or capability after release. The explicit uncertainty must accompany the warning language.
+
+Exhibit 810 physical page 3 / printed 5 also contains information attributed to a redacted source, including reported extremist beliefs and foreign travel. No matching source-description paragraph was identified in the two fully read August 19 court copies. That bounded textual difference does not establish when the information first reached any agency, whether it existed elsewhere, or why a document included it.
+
+A smaller wording difference also remains: the August 19 opening pages describe typical trainee experience as several thousand flight hours; 810 physical page 1 says at least two thousand. These are document statements, not independently verified aviation requirements. Introductory training-payment estimates of $8,000–$9,000 and later $8,300 statements also recur; both scopes are retained rather than a preferred amount selected. No new financial identity or wrongdoing inference follows from their repetition.
+
+## Page order and completeness stay unresolved
+
+Exhibit 810 has five page images: an opening sheet followed by printed pages **2, 5, 6 and 7**. Printed pages 3–4 are not present as page images. The complete local memorandum, source-to-exhibit assembly records and the reason for that gap remain TODOs. The August 19 pages have not been inserted into the August 31 file or treated as replacements for its missing pages.
+
+Exhibit 692 has seven images with an opening sheet and visible printed pages 2–7. Exhibit 688 has five images, but its footer numerals are faint. One 240-dpi footer-reading pass did not justify a complete definitive number sequence. There is also a non-adjacent continuation: the door-interest paragraph near the end of 688 physical page 2 continues at the start of physical page 4. This is a specific court-copy ordering question. No cause or preferred reconstructed order is adopted, and the original PDF order is unchanged. Comparisons use physical PDF page numbers throughout; [PAGE-MAP.json](PAGE-MAP.json) preserves the confidence limits.
+
+## Relation to the OIG account and missing records
+
+The previously read [DOJ OIG account](https://oig.justice.gov/sites/default/files/archive/special/s0606/chapter4.htm), derived paragraphs 327–336, describes a seven-page local assessment, a September 4 instruction to supply an eleven-page headquarters teletype instead, and a September 5 local FAA briefing. The 810 assessment closely corresponds in content; this does not authenticate its full source/version or identify all people behind the OIG's labels. The reported local briefing remains contrary evidence to a claim of total nonwarning. The August 31 document date and September 4 instruction are separate events, not automatically conflicting dates for one act.
+
+Recover the full 810 source and assembly history, original seven-page local memorandum, eleven-page teletype, dispatch/receipt and September 5 briefing/onward-action records. For 688, recover the parallel-criminal-investigation request, routing and actual decision rather than infer refusal from the request. For 692, recover the information-request distribution and responses. The original May 2003 correction and later administrative disposition remain separate unfinished tasks.
+
+## Reading, preservation and continuation
+
+This is a selected claim/paragraph comparison after reading all 17 held page images, not an exhaustive character diff, an independent experiment or native custody authentication. The image-only PDFs returned zero extracted text characters. No OCR or redaction reconstruction was used. Raw sources, higher-resolution reading derivatives and their original order remain preserved locally; the public notes exclude raw scans, signatures, account/contact/identification data and private correspondence. [Source pins](SOURCES.json), [reading/check scope](REVIEW-AND-CHECKS.json), [TODOs and next ready task](OPEN-TODOS.json), and [exact text additions](TEXT-CHANGES.diff) accompany the findings.
+
+Missing inputs remain TODOs with revisit triggers. The next available source task is the court's September 10 memorandum, exhibit 701, indexed as documenting Minneapolis requests to headquarters. Its actual contents, completeness and relevant responses must be inspected before any inference. This comparison does not duplicate recorder/materials work or close the five-track investigation.
